# KNOWN GAP (`todo/t0953`, cell B) — a PACKED `Callable` LOCAL at a plain
# call-argument position leaks the clone the argument marshalling made for it.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS COVERAGE FOR THE ITEM'S AMENDED SCOPE, NOT A PIN. No partial revert
# of R50 Track E's change turns this row red — Track E drains only at the three
# builtin-HOF expanders, and this shape fires at the PLAIN-CALL site, which is
# deliberately not drained. It exists because `t0953`'s scope was amended to
# cover this cell and the Cardinal rule owes every filed cell a durable repro.
#
# THE CELL. `t0953`'s original text named "the closure LITERAL argument". A
# `Callable` LOCAL leaks identically and by a DIFFERENT mechanism: `g` itself is
# freed by GIR drop, but the argument position deep-clones it
# (`gorget_closure_clone_to_owned` mints a FRESH env block) and nothing frees
# the clone. So a GIR-level fix aimed at the literal would convert a case-2 leak
# into a case-2a leak rather than closing the class.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT FIXED HERE. Draining at the plain-call site turns this leak into
# a use-after-free and a double-free, because a callee may RETAIN a borrowed
# `Callable` — `lib/std/iter.gg`'s six lazy adapters do, which is `todo/t1349`,
# whose own write site is `todo/t1350`. Measured, not assumed: the naive drain
# moved `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` from `corruption: 1` to `corruption: 5`.
#
# ⊕ The clone is ALSO gratuitous at a borrowing call (the CoW charter's
# optimality half): a plain-call `Callable` parameter is simply borrowed, so no
# copy is owed at all.
#
# HEAP-FORCED: the closure CAPTURES `base`, so both the local's env and the
# argument clone are real allocations.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the #[ignore]d wired test): prints `41` and runs
# ASan-CLEAN. The stdout is already correct today — the LEAK is the gap, so this
# repro means nothing outside the sanitizer.

int apply(Callable[int(int)] f, int x):
    return f(x)

void main():
    int base = 40
    Callable[int(int)] g = (int n): n + base
    print(f"{apply(g, 1)}")
